00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.030
So there's a whole bunch of
different inputs and outputs.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:10.470
And actually, let me open
up this in another tab.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:15.028
I'll just went up
there because there

00:00:15.028 --> 00:00:17.320
are many examples for the
different inputs and outputs.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.510
So here we have inputs.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.202
So what we saw before
was the slider input.

00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:24.660
And if you click
on this over here,

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.120
you'll see a really
basic example of that.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.475
But there's also text input,
so I can enter texture.

00:00:29.475 --> 00:00:31.920
Let's say , hello shiny.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.280
And this example app will
show how to use that.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.570
These numeric input.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:45.070
So it's similar to the text
input but uses a number input

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:49.050
and there's a
little arrows on it.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.710
Checkbox input, which you
can just turn on and off

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:53.700
by checking it--

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.103
or sorry, by clicking on it.

00:00:55.103 --> 00:00:56.770
And there's a whole
bunch of other ones.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.040
So there's groups of checkboxes.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.209
There's radio buttons.

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:06.090
There's a select input
that has a dropdown

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:11.550
box and dates and date ranges.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:17.760
So these are the built-in
inputs for shiny.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.100
With all these inputs, they
all have a common format,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.023
where you say
ui.input something.

00:01:24.023 --> 00:01:25.440
And then the first
thing that it's

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.607
going to have here, the
first argument that it takes

00:01:27.607 --> 00:01:31.210
is an ID, and then
a label for it.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:35.200
And then there's typically
other parameters as well.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.670
So in this case, we're not using
any of the other parameters.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:42.540
So if we go to the
slider input, you'll

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.810
have to set the minimum
and maximum value

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.110
for the slider, as well as
well as the starting value.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.610
One thing that you
can do with these

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:54.000
is, if you have an idea
of the kind of components

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.980
that you want to add
to your application is.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.080
Let's say, we're working
on this application here,

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.340
and we want to add a
checkbox to it, if we want

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.100
to be able to negate the value.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.600
So I can say, well, I'll put a
comma there just because that

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:10.892
will help this auto completion.

00:02:10.892 --> 00:02:16.500
I can say ui.input_checkbox,
and then parenthesis,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:24.570
and then I can give it a name
like negate and Negate slider

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.320
value.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:27.330
I'll use that as a label.

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.560
And I can click on
this button and it

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.810
will provide this
checkbox, which

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.140
right now doesn't do anything.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.620
We need to actually use
it in our application.

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.500
So let's see.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.620
So let's say-- how
would we do this here?

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:54.480
So we simply say if
input.negate, then

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.690
call this multiplier
equals negative 1

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:06.600
else multiplier equals 1.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.505
And then I can put this here.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.980
And if I rerun this,
then this will work here.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.700
No, I didn't actually fix the
texture with this n times 2.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.360
But you can see how this works
that just simply clicking

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.960
on this will cause
this code to re-execute

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.790
and this value will be updated.

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.720
And, again, if there's
other types of inputs

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.380
that we want to use, we can just
refer to this examples page.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.170
Here I'm in another
browser tab, and I can just

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.030
look for different
types of inputs to use.

